@meta
id: 07-03
title: The same jump from the command line
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: goto-line, find-f
par: 86
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-G, or clicking the line number in a stack trace
open: order.resolver.ts

@brief
Three notes from a code review on this 110 line resolver.

  1. Line 27 — `take: args.take,` should fall back when the client sends
     nothing: `take: args.take ?? 50,`.

  2. Line 71 — the cancel call is not awaited. `const order = this.` should
     read `const order = await this.`.

  3. The file uses the `Order` type and never imports it. Add, as a new
     first line of the file:

         import { Order } from '../../types';

Do them in that order and think about why the order matters.

There is a second way to say "go to line 27", and it is the one you will
reach for when the number is sitting in your other hand — pasted from a
build log, read off a review comment, dictated over a call.

@keys
| `:27` `Enter` | go to line 27 |
| `:1` `Enter` | the first line |
| `:$` `Enter` | the last line |
| `f=` `w` | to the equals sign, then to the start of the next word |
| `O` | open a line above |

@hints
A line number typed on the command line is a command in its own right. You
do not need a verb in front of it.
--
`:27` `Enter` goes to line 27, exactly as `27G` does. `:1` and `:$` are the
two ends. Once the colon is down, the numbers are just digits, so this is
the form that survives being read aloud or pasted.
--
On line 27 the comma is the anchor: `f,` then `i` to write in front of it.
On line 71 you need to get in front of `this`, and `f=` then `w` lands you
exactly there. Leave the import until last.

@solution
    :27<Enter> f , i  ?? 50 <Esc>
    :71<Enter> f = w i await  <Esc>
    :1<Enter> O import { Order } from '../../types'; <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

About seventy-five keystrokes, fifty of them the import line.

`:27` and `27G` land in the same place, and which you use is mostly about
where the number came from. `27G` is quicker under the fingers. `:27` wins
when you are copying a number from somewhere else, because the colon puts
you in a mode where digits are digits and a typo is fixable with Backspace
rather than being executed as a command the moment you press it. `:$` is
also the only one of the two ends that reads as plainly as it behaves.

Now the ordering. The import goes in first in the finished file but you
typed it last, because inserting a line at the top pushes every line below
it down by one and the review's numbers would all have been wrong by one.
Line numbers are a snapshot, not an address. When you have a list of them
to work through, go from the bottom of the file upwards, or make the
insertions that shift things last. This is the single most common way
people mangle a file while fixing it from a list of compiler errors.

@start order.resolver.ts
import { Resolver, Query, Mutation, Args } from 'type-graphql';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { RefundProvider } from '../refund/refund.provider';

@injectable()
@Resolver()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(RefundProvider) protected refundProvider: RefundProvider,
  ) {}

  @Query(() => Order)
  public async order(@Args() args: OrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return order;
  }

  @Query(() => [Order])
  public async orders(@Args() args: OrdersArgs): Promise<Order[]> {
    const orders = await this.orderProvider.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId },
      take: args.take,
    });

    return orders;
  }

  @Query(() => [Order])
  public async pendingOrders(@Args() args: PendingOrdersArgs): Promise<Order[]> {
    const orders = await this.orderProvider.findPending(args.take ?? 25);

    return orders;
  }

  @Query(() => Customer)
  public async orderCustomer(@Args() args: OrderArgs): Promise<Customer> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
    const customer = await this.customerProvider.findOne(order.customerId);

    return customer;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async createOrder(@Args() args: CreateOrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.create({
      customerId: args.customerId,
      amount: args.amount,
      currency: args.currency ?? 'GBP',
    });

    return order;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async updateOrder(@Args() args: UpdateOrderInput): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.update({
      where: { id: args.id },
      values: { amount: args.amount },
    });

    return order;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async cancelOrder(@Args() args: CancelOrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);

    return order;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Refund)
  public async refundOrder(@Args() args: RefundOrderArgs): Promise<Refund> {
    const refund = await this.refundProvider.refund({
      orderId: args.orderId,
      amount: args.amount,
      reason: args.reason,
    });

    return refund;
  }

  @Query(() => [Order])
  public async cancelledOrders(@Args() args: OrdersArgs): Promise<Order[]> {
    const orders = await this.orderProvider.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, status: 'CANCELLED' },
      take: args.take ?? 25,
    });

    return orders;
  }

  @Query(() => Number)
  public async orderCount(@Args() args: OrderCountArgs): Promise<number> {
    const count = await this.orderProvider.countByStatus(args.status);

    return count;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async reinstateOrder(@Args() args: OrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.reinstate(args.id);

    return order;
  }
}

@expected order.resolver.ts
import { Order } from '../../types';
import { Resolver, Query, Mutation, Args } from 'type-graphql';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { RefundProvider } from '../refund/refund.provider';

@injectable()
@Resolver()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(RefundProvider) protected refundProvider: RefundProvider,
  ) {}

  @Query(() => Order)
  public async order(@Args() args: OrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return order;
  }

  @Query(() => [Order])
  public async orders(@Args() args: OrdersArgs): Promise<Order[]> {
    const orders = await this.orderProvider.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId },
      take: args.take ?? 50,
    });

    return orders;
  }

  @Query(() => [Order])
  public async pendingOrders(@Args() args: PendingOrdersArgs): Promise<Order[]> {
    const orders = await this.orderProvider.findPending(args.take ?? 25);

    return orders;
  }

  @Query(() => Customer)
  public async orderCustomer(@Args() args: OrderArgs): Promise<Customer> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
    const customer = await this.customerProvider.findOne(order.customerId);

    return customer;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async createOrder(@Args() args: CreateOrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.create({
      customerId: args.customerId,
      amount: args.amount,
      currency: args.currency ?? 'GBP',
    });

    return order;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async updateOrder(@Args() args: UpdateOrderInput): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.update({
      where: { id: args.id },
      values: { amount: args.amount },
    });

    return order;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async cancelOrder(@Args() args: CancelOrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);

    return order;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Refund)
  public async refundOrder(@Args() args: RefundOrderArgs): Promise<Refund> {
    const refund = await this.refundProvider.refund({
      orderId: args.orderId,
      amount: args.amount,
      reason: args.reason,
    });

    return refund;
  }

  @Query(() => [Order])
  public async cancelledOrders(@Args() args: OrdersArgs): Promise<Order[]> {
    const orders = await this.orderProvider.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, status: 'CANCELLED' },
      take: args.take ?? 25,
    });

    return orders;
  }

  @Query(() => Number)
  public async orderCount(@Args() args: OrderCountArgs): Promise<number> {
    const count = await this.orderProvider.countByStatus(args.status);

    return count;
  }

  @Mutation(() => Order)
  public async reinstateOrder(@Args() args: OrderArgs): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.reinstate(args.id);

    return order;
  }
}
